    <section>
      <h2>23. Project Numbers in the App Overview</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>“Get involved”</strong> section of the app's overview shows how many stars WhisPaste has on GitHub and how many subscribers its YouTube channel has. The app asks our own API <code>api.whispaste.de</code> for these numbers (netcup GmbH, Germany, see section&nbsp;17), never GitHub or YouTube directly. It asks at most once a day, and only while the overview with this section or the support page with the star count is open. If you hide the section (“Hide this section” or under “Customise overview”), the app no longer fetches the numbers, neither on the overview nor on the support page.
      </p>
      <p>
        The request carries no identifier, no account, no settings and no content from the app. For technical reasons the server receives your IP address in order to deliver the answer. It appears in the web server's access log, which is deleted after 14&nbsp;days at the latest. To limit the number of requests per sender, the API only uses a hash of the IP address formed with a secret key, which expires after one minute. The answer contains only the two numbers and may be cached for up to one hour. The API itself asks GitHub and YouTube (Google) at most every 3&nbsp;hours and passes on no data about you. The legal basis is Art.&nbsp;6(1)(f) GDPR (legitimate interest in showing current project figures).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="newsletter">
      <h2>24. Newsletter</h2>
      <p>
        You can sign up for the WhisPaste newsletter on the website or in the app. For this we process your email address and first send you a confirmation email (double opt-in). You are only on the list once you confirm the link in it within 48&nbsp;hours. If you do not confirm, the link expires and we delete the sign-up at the next clean-up. Nothing is transmitted unless you submit the sign-up.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> news about updates, tips and insights around WhisPaste.</li>
        <li><strong>Legal basis:</strong> your consent (Art.&nbsp;6(1)(a) GDPR).</li>
        <li><strong>Data stored:</strong> your email address (stored encrypted), the chosen language, where you signed up (website or app), the times of sign-up and confirmation, the version of the consent text, and an irreversible hash of your IP address formed with a secret key. We use these to prove your consent.</li>
        <li><strong>Protection against abuse:</strong> so that nobody can flood other people's addresses with confirmation emails, we limit the number of sign-ups per IP address and per email address. An invisible check field and the time taken to fill in the form help to detect automated sign-ups; no further data is stored for this. The API's technical log contains neither the email address nor the IP address and is deleted after 14&nbsp;days.</li>
        <li><strong>Confirmation in the app:</strong> If you sign up in the app, it creates a random status code and sends it with the sign-up. We store only an irreversible hash of it, formed with a secret key. The code itself stays in the app's settings on your device; it only reveals whether the sign-up from this device was confirmed, which is why it is not kept in the operating system's credential store. While the sign-up waits for your confirmation, at most 48&nbsp;hours, the app uses this code to ask <code>api.whispaste.de</code> whether you have confirmed: when the “Get involved” section appears or the app window comes to the front, at most every two minutes, and right away when the confirmation page sends you back to the app. Neither your email address nor any other information is sent to us in the process. Once you have confirmed, our server reports this exactly once and deletes the hash; the app deletes the code and keeps only the time of confirmation. Without confirmation, both delete it after 48&nbsp;hours. The legal basis is your consent (Art.&nbsp;6(1)(a) GDPR). So that the check cannot be abused, we limit the number of checks per code and per IP address; for this we store both only as a hash formed with a secret key and only for the current one-hour window (Art.&nbsp;6(1)(f) GDPR, protection against abuse).</li>
        <li><strong>Welcome email:</strong> Once you confirm, we send you a single welcome email with the first steps and a tip. It rests on the same consent, contains no tracking pixels and triggers no further storage.</li>
        <li><strong>Already signed up:</strong> If you sign up again in the app with an address that is already confirmed, we send you the email “You are already subscribed to WhisPaste news”. To limit how many emails we send you, we store for this, for at most 48&nbsp;hours, an irreversible hash of the app’s device key, the time and count of the last such email, and the start of the current 30-day period. Only when you click “Connect the app” in that email does the app learn that your address is signed up.</li>
        <li><strong>Language from the app:</strong> If you signed up in the app or linked it to your sign-up via “Connect the app”, we store an irreversible hash of the app’s device key until your sign-up ends. It is used only to adopt the app’s language for your newsletter emails when you change it in the app; the app does not need your address for this. It is deleted as soon as you unsubscribe, your address becomes undeliverable or your sign-up ends. If you sign up again in another language, we write to you in that language from then on.</li>
        <li><strong>No tracking:</strong> we do not measure whether you open emails or click links. The emails contain no tracking pixels, no tracking parameters and no remotely loaded images.</li>
        <li><strong>Sending:</strong> through our own mail server at netcup GmbH in Germany (processor under Art.&nbsp;28 GDPR). We do not pass your data on to third parties.</li>
        <li><strong>Withdrawal and unsubscribing:</strong> you can withdraw your consent at any time through the unsubscribe link in every email. We then delete your address and language. So that we send you nothing more, only an irreversible keyed value of the address stays on a suppression list. We keep the proof of your consent (times, version of the consent text, where you signed up) for three years and then delete it automatically, together with the suppression entry. The withdrawal does not affect the lawfulness of the emails sent before it (Art.&nbsp;7(3) GDPR). For the backups of our host, see section&nbsp;17.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="weather">
      <h2>25. Weather in the App Overview</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>“Weather”</strong> card on the app’s overview is off by default. You switch it on under “Customise overview” and then choose a place yourself. WhisPaste does not use your device’s location services for this.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Place search:</strong> When you search for a place, the app sends your search term and the app’s language to the Nominatim place search of the OpenStreetMap Foundation (United Kingdom, covered by an adequacy decision of the EU Commission). It searches only when you click “Search” or press Enter, never while you type, and at most once per second. See the <a href="https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">privacy policy of the OpenStreetMap Foundation</a>. Place data © OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Forecast:</strong> For the forecast, the app sends the coordinates of the chosen place, rounded to two decimals (about one kilometre), directly to the weather API of the Norwegian Meteorological Institute (MET Norway, Oslo, Norway, within the European Economic Area). It asks at most about once an hour, only once the last forecast has expired, and only while the overview is visible; it never asks in the background or without a chosen place. When offline, the card stays quiet and shows the last forecast.
      </p>
      <p>
        Neither request carries an identifier, an account or any content from the app, only the app’s name and version and our website address as a contact, as both services require. For technical reasons both services receive your IP address. The chosen place (name and rounded coordinates) and the last forecast are kept only in the app’s settings on your device; “Remove place” deletes both. The weather data comes from MET Norway under the CC&nbsp;BY&nbsp;4.0 licence. The legal basis is Art.&nbsp;6(1)(f) GDPR (legitimate interest in providing the weather display you switched on).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="news">
      <h2>26. News in the App Overview</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>“News”</strong> block on the app’s overview is off by default. You switch it on under “Customise overview” and decide under “Sources” which RSS or Atom feeds it shows, up to five. The feed of our blog is suggested (<code>whispaste.de/blog/feed.xml</code>, see section&nbsp;3); you enter any other address yourself.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Fetching:</strong> The app loads each chosen feed directly from its server: once when you add it, then at most once an hour, and only while the overview shows the block and the app window is active. It never asks at start-up, in the background or without a feed. The request is a plain fetch of the feed address with “WhisPaste” as the program name, without a device identifier, without a cookie and without any content from the app. For technical reasons the feed’s server receives your IP address; the privacy policy of its operator applies to it. If you enter an address with <code>http://</code>, the fetch is unencrypted.
      </p>
      <p>
        The list of your feeds and the most recently loaded entries (title, source, date, link) are kept only in the app’s database on your device. When you remove a feed, the app also deletes its entries. Clicking an entry opens it in your browser. The legal basis is Art.&nbsp;6(1)(f) GDPR (legitimate interest in providing the news you switched on).
      </p>
    </section>
